window.ECBA_QUESTION_BANK=window.ECBA_QUESTION_BANK||[];window.ECBA_QUESTION_BANK.push(...[{"id":"q189","domains":[3],"level":3,"tag":"Value","q":"A benefits case assumes users adopt a new process immediately, but training capacity is limited. Which change-strategy decision best protects value?","opts":["Keep the full launch date and treat training as a separate operational issue","Launch to every group at once so benefits begin as early as possible","Delay all change until training capacity is unlimited","Choose a phased transition after assessing readiness, dependencies, risk, and the timing of benefit realization"],"answer":[3],"why":["Training readiness affects adoption and benefit realization; excluding it can undermine the change strategy.","A broad launch may accelerate some benefits but can increase risk when readiness is limited.","Unlimited capacity may not be feasible; the BA should assess practical transition alternatives.","Correct: consider transition options in light of readiness, dependencies, risk, and when benefits can realistically be achieved."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §6.4 Define Change Strategy, printed pp. 124–132","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-20.html"},{"id":"q190","domains":[3],"level":3,"tag":"Value","q":"Two transition options can reach the same future state. One is faster but creates a long period of duplicate work; the other is slower but reduces operational disruption. What should inform the recommendation?","opts":["Compare transition costs, risks, readiness, stakeholder impacts, and timing of expected value","Select the faster option because earlier value always outweighs temporary cost","Select the slower option because lower disruption always maximizes value","Ask the implementation team to choose based on its preferred schedule"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: compare the alternatives using relevant value, transition, risk, readiness, and stakeholder considerations.","Earlier value is important but does not automatically outweigh duplicate-work costs and risks.","Lower disruption is valuable but does not automatically outweigh all other impacts and benefits.","Implementation input is important, but the recommendation should reflect agreed business and stakeholder criteria."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §6.4 Define Change Strategy, printed pp. 124–132","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-20.html"},{"id":"q191","domains":[3],"level":3,"tag":"Value","q":"A requirement is complete and unambiguous, but stakeholders say satisfying it would not achieve the intended benefit. What should the BA conclude?","opts":["The requirement is valid because it is well written","The requirement is verified, but its contribution to the need and desired value still requires validation","The requirement is approved because stakeholders reviewed it","The benefit should be removed from the business case to preserve the requirement"],"answer":[1],"why":["Clarity and completeness support quality but do not prove that the requirement contributes to the business need.","Correct: verification concerns requirement quality; validation checks whether requirements support the need and desired outcomes.","Review does not itself establish approval or value alignment.","A mismatch between requirement and benefit calls for analysis, not removal of the outcome to protect the requirement."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.3 Validate Requirements, printed pp. 144–147","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-20.html"},{"id":"q192","domains":[3],"level":3,"tag":"Value","q":"Several individually acceptable requirements collectively exceed the cost limit and leave a key stakeholder outcome unmet. What is the most useful validation action?","opts":["Validate each requirement separately and treat total cost as a delivery issue","Ask the sponsor to approve the set because the requirements have been reviewed","Assess the requirements together against the business need, desired outcomes, and constraints; identify gaps or conflicts","Remove the lowest-priority requirement without assessing its contribution to the unmet outcome"],"answer":[2],"why":["Individual quality does not establish that the full set produces feasible value.","Review and approval do not replace validation against the need and constraints.","Correct: assess whether the set collectively supports the desired outcomes within constraints and identify what is missing or conflicting.","A low-priority item may still contribute to the unmet outcome; assess the impact before removing it."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.3 Validate Requirements, printed pp. 144–147","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-20.html"},{"id":"q193","domains":[3],"level":4,"tag":"Value","q":"A solution option has the highest expected benefit, but the estimate depends on an untested adoption assumption. A second option has lower but more certain benefit. What should the BA recommend?","opts":["Select the higher estimate because maximizing expected value is the only relevant criterion","Select the more certain option because uncertain benefits should not be considered","Delay the recommendation until adoption is certain","Compare value, assumptions, risk, and evidence against the agreed criteria, and explain the trade-offs in the recommendation"],"answer":[3],"why":["Expected benefit matters, but the untested assumption may materially change the estimate.","Uncertainty should be assessed and managed; it does not automatically disqualify an option.","Certainty may not be attainable before a decision; additional evidence or mitigation may be compared with the cost of delay.","Correct: assess potential value transparently, including assumptions and risks, then recommend using the decision criteria."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution, printed pp. 157–165","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-20.html"},{"id":"q194","domains":[3],"level":4,"tag":"Value","q":"A design meets the requirements and has strong financial return, but shifts substantial effort to a partner organization. Which analysis is needed before recommending it?","opts":["Assess value and impacts across affected stakeholders, including transferred costs and feasible mitigations","Treat the partner's effort as outside the solution's value assessment","Recommend it based on financial return and let the partner negotiate impacts later","Reject it because any transferred effort makes a solution unacceptable"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: compare potential value and impacts across affected parties, including costs, risks, and possible mitigations.","A dependency or transferred impact can affect overall value even if outside the immediate implementation boundary.","Deferring known impacts prevents an informed recommendation and may undermine the expected return.","Transferred effort is important but does not automatically make the option unacceptable; assess scale and mitigations."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.6 Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution, printed pp. 157–165","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-20.html"},{"id":"q195","domains":[3],"level":4,"tag":"Value","q":"A solution meets its cost target, but the expected customer outcome has not improved. Which evaluation approach best determines performance?","opts":["Report the cost target as success because it was the main constraint","Measure only customer satisfaction because the financial result is known","Compare relevant performance measures with the intended outcomes and baselines, then interpret the results in context","Wait for stakeholders to request an evaluation before gathering more data"],"answer":[2],"why":["Meeting one constraint does not show that the solution achieved its intended customer outcome.","Satisfaction may be relevant, but one measure may not explain performance or the unmet outcome.","Correct: measure solution performance against desired outcomes and baselines, then interpret evidence in context.","Evaluation should use planned and relevant measures; waiting can delay discovery of an important value gap."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.1 Measure Solution Performance, printed pp. 166–169","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-20.html"},{"id":"q196","domains":[3],"level":4,"tag":"Value","q":"A service solution increases completed transactions, but repeat contacts and accessibility complaints also rise. What is the best performance conclusion?","opts":["The transaction target proves the solution is delivering value","The other indicators should be reviewed alongside the target using measures tied to the desired outcomes","The solution is failing because any complaint outweighs all positive measures","The increase in repeat contacts should be excluded if it was not in the original dashboard"],"answer":[1],"why":["One target can improve while other important outcomes worsen, so it is insufficient alone.","Correct: assess a relevant set of measures against desired outcomes, including unintended effects and stakeholder impacts.","Complaints are evidence to investigate but do not automatically outweigh all other performance evidence.","Relevant evidence should not be ignored merely because a measure was omitted from the initial dashboard; assess the gap in measurement."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.1 Measure Solution Performance, printed pp. 166–169","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-20.html"},{"id":"q197","domains":[3],"level":5,"tag":"Value","q":"Across multiple initiatives, teams report that stakeholder review takes too long, but review quality varies widely. Which improvement recommendation is strongest?","opts":["Set a single shorter review deadline for every stakeholder group","Replace stakeholder reviews with automated checks wherever possible","Compare causes and outcomes across initiatives, then pilot a targeted improvement and measure its effect on speed and quality","Rank teams by review time and require the slowest team to copy the fastest"],"answer":[2],"why":["A uniform deadline may speed some reviews but harm quality or ignore different causes.","Automation may help some checks but cannot replace stakeholder judgment in every context.","Correct: use performance evidence to identify causes, try a suitable improvement, and assess its impact on relevant measures.","The fastest team's context may differ, and copying its process without analysis may not improve quality or value."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §3.5 Identify Business Analysis Performance Improvements, printed pp. 47–55","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-20.html"},{"id":"q198","domains":[3],"level":5,"tag":"Value","q":"A solution has high adoption but yields less benefit than expected because a manual approval bottleneck remains. What should the BA recommend?","opts":["Increase adoption targets so more users enter the process","Investigate the value gap and its cause, then assess targeted actions for the approval bottleneck with stakeholders","Replace the solution because adoption has not produced the expected benefit","Close evaluation because the adoption target was achieved"],"answer":[1],"why":["More adoption may increase workload at the bottleneck without addressing the cause of the shortfall.","Correct: investigate why expected value was not achieved and assess feasible actions connected to the identified cause.","Replacement may be considered, but selecting it before analyzing the bottleneck may be premature.","Adoption is not the same as benefit realization; the value gap remains important."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value, printed pp. 182–187","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-20.html"},{"id":"q199","domains":[3],"level":5,"tag":"Value","q":"Training, a policy change, and a solution enhancement could each improve value, but evidence about the primary cause is incomplete. What is the best next step?","opts":["Recommend the least expensive action immediately","Implement all three actions to cover every possible cause","Ask the solution vendor to select the most appropriate action","Gather focused evidence to distinguish causes, then compare actions by expected value, risk, cost, and stakeholder impact"],"answer":[3],"why":["Low cost does not establish that training addresses the actual cause.","Implementing all actions may be costly and creates change without evidence of need.","Vendor expertise may inform product options but cannot replace analysis of policy and stakeholder factors.","Correct: reduce relevant uncertainty and compare feasible actions against value and impact before recommending."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value, printed pp. 182–187","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-20.html"},{"id":"q200","domains":[3],"level":5,"tag":"Value","q":"An improvement could increase throughput while transferring ongoing costs to a partner and increasing support burden for frontline staff. What should the recommendation include?","opts":["Expected benefits and impacts across affected stakeholders, including cost transfers, support effects, risks, and possible mitigations","Throughput alone because it is the measured business case outcome","Partner cost only because it is the newly identified impact","A rejection of the improvement because the change creates any additional burden"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: evaluate overall value across stakeholder impacts and trade-offs, including transferred cost and support burden, and consider mitigations.","Throughput alone can hide material cost and workload shifts that alter net value.","Partner cost is one impact, but frontline support effects and the expected throughput benefit also matter.","Additional burden may be acceptable or mitigable; assess its magnitude and trade-offs before deciding."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.5 Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value, printed pp. 182–187","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-20.html"},{"id":"q201","domains":[3],"level":1,"tag":"Context","q":"A small initiative has stable needs, a strict approval process, and a fixed delivery date. Which basis should guide the BA approach?","opts":["The initiative's context, including its change characteristics, governance, risks, and constraints","The approach used by the BA team on its most recent project","The sponsor's preferred technique, regardless of the delivery context","A fully adaptive approach because requirements may change in any initiative"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: select and tailor the business analysis approach to the initiative's context, including governance, risk, constraints, and change characteristics.","A prior approach may provide a starting point but may not fit this initiative's context.","Sponsor preferences matter, but the approach must also fit the needs, risks, and constraints of the work.","Some change is possible in any initiative; that alone does not establish that a fully adaptive approach is appropriate."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach, printed pp. 24–30","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q202","domains":[3],"level":1,"tag":"Context","q":"A BA approach was approved before the team learned that a key vendor will deliver information in a different format and cadence. What should the BA do?","opts":["Keep the approved approach unchanged so governance remains stable","Reassess the approach in light of the new context and determine whether planned activities, timing, or techniques need adjustment","Ask the vendor to follow the original approach without examining feasibility","Replace the entire approach because any context change invalidates the plan"],"answer":[1],"why":["An approved approach may need revision when important contextual assumptions change.","Correct: review how the new information affects the approach and adjust the relevant elements through the applicable governance process.","The vendor constraint is contextual information to assess; assuming it can be ignored may jeopardize the work.","A change may require a targeted adjustment rather than replacing the whole approach."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach, printed pp. 24–30","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q203","domains":[3],"level":1,"tag":"Context","q":"A regulated initiative uses several repositories for requirements, decisions, and designs. Which information-management decision best accounts for context?","opts":["Move all information to one tool immediately, regardless of access or integration needs","Let each analyst choose personal naming and access rules","Define suitable classifications, ownership, access, retention, and links across the repositories","Keep only approved requirements because other information may be outdated"],"answer":[2],"why":["One tool may not fit regulatory, integration, or access constraints and does not by itself define information management.","Personal conventions undermine consistent access, maintenance, and reuse.","Correct: define information-management rules suited to the initiative, including handling and relationships among information items.","Decisions and designs may be needed to understand rationale and context; discarding them risks losing useful information."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management, printed pp. 42–46","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q204","domains":[3],"level":1,"tag":"Context","q":"A team reuses a requirements package from a prior project, but its assumptions and jurisdiction-specific rules differ. What should the BA do before relying on it?","opts":["Use the package as written because it was previously approved","Keep the wording and update only the document date","Ask the original author whether it worked well on the prior project","Assess currency, applicability, source, and context, then adapt or reject information that does not fit"],"answer":[3],"why":["Prior approval applies to a prior context and does not establish suitability here.","Changing a date does not resolve differences in assumptions or rules.","Author feedback may help but does not replace evaluating the information against the current context.","Correct: information should be assessed for relevance and currency in the current context before it is reused."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management, printed pp. 42–46","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q205","domains":[3],"level":2,"tag":"Context","q":"A requirement is reused for a new business unit. The wording is unchanged, but its rationale, owner, and applicable process differ. What maintenance action is most appropriate?","opts":["Retain the existing record unchanged because requirement text is identical","Update the relevant attributes and context links, preserving the requirement's history and relationships","Create a duplicate without linking it to the original","Replace the requirement with a design description that reflects the new process"],"answer":[1],"why":["Unchanged wording does not mean the requirement's context and relationships remain unchanged.","Correct: maintain the requirement so its current attributes and relationships reflect the new context while preserving traceability.","An unlinked duplicate can obscure reuse history and create inconsistency.","A design description is not a substitute for maintaining the requirement and its context."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.2 Maintain Requirements, printed pp. 83–85","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q206","domains":[3],"level":2,"tag":"Context","q":"A requirement repository has lost links between approved requirements and the business objectives they support. Why should the BA restore these links?","opts":["To make the repository appear complete for an audit","To preserve context for understanding rationale, relationships, and the requirement's continuing relevance","To allow the BA to approve related requirements without stakeholders","To avoid revisiting whether the objective has changed"],"answer":[2],"why":["Repository completeness is not the primary reason; the links support meaningful management and use of requirements.","The link does not transfer approval authority from stakeholders to the BA.","Correct: relationships to objectives preserve context and help assess relevance and impacts over the requirement lifecycle.","A link helps assess relevance but does not remove the need to consider changes to the objective."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.2 Maintain Requirements, printed pp. 83–85","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q207","domains":[3],"level":2,"tag":"Context","q":"A requirement is approved for a pilot in one region. Another region has different operating rules and asks to use the same requirement. What is the best response?","opts":["Treat the original approval as enterprise-wide because the requirement wording is the same","Ask the second region to accept the requirement without review","Change the text immediately to mention both regions and retain the pilot approval","Clarify the scope and context of the approval, assess applicability in the second region, and follow the approval process for any needed change"],"answer":[3],"why":["Approval in one context does not necessarily authorize use in another context with different rules.","Acceptance should not be assumed where applicability and approval scope are unresolved.","Changing wording without analysis can obscure the difference and does not ensure the revised requirement is approved.","Correct: establish what was approved, assess the new context, and obtain appropriate review or approval for changes."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.5 Approve Requirements, printed pp. 95–102","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q208","domains":[3],"level":2,"tag":"Context","q":"An authorized approver signs a requirement set, but the record does not identify which version or operating context was reviewed. What should the BA do?","opts":["Clarify and record the approved baseline, version, and applicable context with the approver","Assume the latest repository version is the one approved","Mark all related requirements approved because they share a project","Request a new approval without first determining what the existing approval covered"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: approval records should identify what was reviewed and the context or baseline to which the decision applies.","The latest version may differ from the version the approver actually reviewed.","Related requirements are not automatically approved by association.","First clarify the scope of the existing decision; a new approval may be needed if its coverage cannot be established."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.5 Approve Requirements, printed pp. 95–102","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q209","domains":[3],"level":3,"tag":"Context","q":"A new platform is proposed, but stakeholders describe current-state problems differently across regions. What should the BA do before defining the change?","opts":["Use the enterprise average as the current state for every region","Select the region with the strongest sponsor and treat it as representative","Investigate the current state in its organizational and operational context, identifying commonalities and meaningful differences","Skip current-state analysis because the platform choice is already proposed"],"answer":[2],"why":["An average can hide regional differences that affect the need and value of change.","Sponsor influence does not establish that one region represents the others.","Correct: understand the current state in context and distinguish shared conditions from material variation.","A proposed solution does not remove the need to understand the current state and its causes."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §6.1 Analyze Current State, printed pp. 103–109","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q210","domains":[3],"level":3,"tag":"Context","q":"A process has a high error rate, but errors occur mainly during a seasonal volume spike. What is the most useful current-state analysis?","opts":["Use the annual average error rate and ignore timing","Examine performance patterns, conditions, and contributing factors across normal and peak periods","Assume the spike is caused by staff performance and recommend retraining","Compare only the process design document with the proposed platform"],"answer":[1],"why":["An annual average can conceal the context in which errors occur and their causes.","Correct: analyze current performance in context, including variation and conditions that influence the observed result.","The cause has not been established; assuming staff performance may lead to an ineffective change.","A design document alone does not show how the process performs in actual operating conditions."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §6.1 Analyze Current State, printed pp. 103–109","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q211","domains":[3],"level":3,"tag":"Context","q":"A requirement describes a report but omits the data cutoff time that users rely on during month-end. What should the BA do during verification?","opts":["Check the requirement against quality criteria and the relevant context, then resolve the missing or ambiguous detail","Add the cutoff time as an assumption and treat the requirement as verified","Ask users to accept the report requirement because they requested it","Validate the report's business value and skip the wording review"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: verify the requirement's quality and completeness in context, clarifying information needed for consistent interpretation.","An assumption does not resolve a material ambiguity that affects whether the requirement is understood consistently.","A request does not establish that the requirement is complete or clear.","Validation of value and verification of requirement quality address different questions; both may be needed."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.2 Verify Requirements, printed pp. 141–143","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q212","domains":[3],"level":3,"tag":"Context","q":"A requirement is internally consistent but uses a term that has different meanings in two business units. Which verification concern is most directly raised?","opts":["The requirement may be ambiguous in its context and should be clarified or qualified","The requirement is invalid because the units have different processes","The requirement is approved because it is consistent within itself","The solution architecture is incomplete because the term is not standardized"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: context-specific meanings can make a requirement ambiguous, so terminology or scope should be clarified.","Different processes do not automatically make the requirement invalid; assess its intended scope and need.","Consistency does not establish approval or resolve the differing interpretations.","The issue is requirement meaning; architecture may be affected later but is not the direct verification concern."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.2 Verify Requirements, printed pp. 141–143","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q213","domains":[3],"level":4,"tag":"Context","q":"A set of requirements is organized by department, but several requirements affect the same customer journey across departments. What should the BA consider in the requirements architecture?","opts":["Keep the departmental structure as the only view because ownership is clear","Combine all requirements into one list to remove structural differences","Model relevant relationships and provide views that reflect the cross-department context and the needs of different stakeholders","Move the customer journey into a design document and leave requirements separate"],"answer":[3],"why":["Department ownership may be useful, but alone it hides cross-boundary relationships.","A single undifferentiated list can make relationships and structure harder to understand.","The design document may describe a solution, but does not replace structuring and relating requirements.","Correct: requirements architecture organizes requirements and their relationships in ways that fit stakeholder perspectives and the solution context."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.4 Define Requirements Architecture, printed pp. 148–151","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q214","domains":[3],"level":4,"tag":"Context","q":"A data requirement appears in two system components with different terminology. What is the best architectural treatment?","opts":["Leave both unconnected because each component has its own vocabulary","Represent the relationship and clarify whether the terms refer to the same concept, related concepts, or distinct needs","Rename both terms to match the first component without consulting stakeholders","Remove one requirement to prevent duplication"],"answer":[1],"why":["Separate vocabularies do not remove the need to understand relationships across components.","Correct: make the relationship visible and resolve meaning in context before deciding whether the requirements are duplicates or distinct.","Unilateral renaming can change meaning and may not reflect stakeholder usage.","The requirements may represent distinct needs; deletion without analysis can lose a requirement."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.4 Define Requirements Architecture, printed pp. 148–151","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q215","domains":[3],"level":4,"tag":"Context","q":"A solution cannot process a particular data format required by a stakeholder group. The enterprise could change its upstream system, but that option is costly. How should the BA classify and assess the issue?","opts":["Assess it as a solution limitation and determine its effect on value, context, and possible responses","Classify it as an enterprise limitation because the upstream system belongs to the enterprise","Treat it as a requirement defect because the solution does not meet the need","Ignore it because an upstream change is technically possible"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: the inability is a limitation of the solution; assess its impact on expected value and consider viable responses in context.","The source system may constrain a response, but the stated inability is in the solution's capability.","A requirement may need review, but that does not change the classification of the solution's limitation.","Technical possibility does not make the cost, feasibility, or impact irrelevant."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.3 Assess Solution Limitations, printed pp. 173–176","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q216","domains":[3],"level":4,"tag":"Context","q":"A solution meets performance targets in testing but struggles with a legacy interface in production. What should the BA do when assessing the limitation?","opts":["Conclude the solution is not limited because test results met targets","Raise the performance target so the production gap is easier to detect","Reclassify the issue as an enterprise limitation without examining the solution behavior","Assess the limitation in the operational context and its impact on value, including the interface dependency"],"answer":[3],"why":["Test results from one context do not establish performance under the production conditions.","Changing a target does not explain the gap or assess the limitation.","The enterprise interface may contribute, but reclassification does not replace analysis of the solution's behavior and impact.","Correct: examine the solution limitation and the conditions and dependencies that shape its impact on realized value."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.3 Assess Solution Limitations, printed pp. 173–176","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q217","domains":[3],"level":5,"tag":"Context","q":"A merger has changed decision rights and duplicated customer records. Teams disagree about whether the main current-state problem is governance or data quality. What should the BA do?","opts":["Define the future state first so the current-state debate does not delay planning","Choose data quality because it is easiest to quantify","Ask the sponsor to pick one cause before analysis continues","Analyze the current state across organizational structures, processes, information, and decision rights to understand how the conditions interact"],"answer":[3],"why":["A future state should be informed by an adequate understanding of the current state and need.","Ease of measurement does not establish that data quality is the main cause or independent of governance.","The sponsor's view may inform analysis but does not replace understanding interacting current-state conditions.","Correct: examine the relevant current-state elements and their relationships to understand the context and contributing causes."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §6.1 Analyze Current State, printed pp. 103–109","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q218","domains":[3],"level":5,"tag":"Context","q":"A solution performs as designed, but benefits are limited by outdated policies and a shortage of trained staff. What should the BA assess?","opts":["Solution limitations only, because the deployed solution is the object being evaluated","Enterprise limitations and how these constraints affect value realization and possible responses","Requirements defects, because expected benefits were not achieved","Performance measures only, because policies and staffing are outside the solution"],"answer":[1],"why":["The solution may work as designed; the stated constraints are within the enterprise context and need separate consideration.","Correct: assess enterprise factors that constrain value and how they affect the ability to realize expected benefits.","A benefit gap does not by itself show that the requirements are defective; examine the enterprise constraints.","Measures may reveal the gap, but they do not assess the policies and workforce conditions constraining value."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations, printed pp. 177–181","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q219","domains":[3],"level":5,"tag":"Context","q":"A regulatory change limits a solution's use in one market, while internal policy limits it in another. How should the BA organize the assessment?","opts":["Combine both into one solution defect because each prevents full use","Assess only the regulatory issue because external constraints have greater authority","Distinguish the enterprise limitations by source and context, then assess their individual and combined effect on potential value","Treat the internal policy as irrelevant until it becomes a law"],"answer":[2],"why":["The constraints may have different owners, causes, and responses even if both restrict use.","External authority does not make the internal constraint immaterial to value realization.","Correct: identify and assess enterprise limitations in context, including how separate constraints interact and affect value.","An internal policy can constrain the enterprise even when it is not a legal requirement."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations, printed pp. 177–181","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q220","domains":[3],"level":5,"tag":"Context","q":"A department could remove a staffing constraint by hiring, but its budget is frozen. Another department can share staff after a policy change. Which conclusion best reflects enterprise-limitation analysis?","opts":["The staffing limitation is permanent because the current budget is frozen","The shared-staff option should be selected because it requires no new budget","Assess the constraints, dependencies, and feasible responses with stakeholders before deciding how much value can be realized","Reclassify staffing as a solution limitation because the solution needs operators"],"answer":[2],"why":["A current budget constraint does not prove that the limitation is permanent or that future responses are impossible.","A policy change may carry costs and impacts; no new hiring does not mean no trade-off.","Correct: assess enterprise constraints and potential responses in context, including dependencies and consequences for value.","The need for staff does not make the staffing constraint a limitation of the solution itself; it is an enterprise condition."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations, printed pp. 177–181","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-21.html"},{"id":"q221","domains":[3],"level":1,"tag":"Context","q":"A cross-border change has stable objectives, but local regulations, approval paths, and stakeholder availability differ by market. What should guide the BA approach?","opts":["Use the same activity sequence in every market to preserve consistency","Use the most adaptive approach available because the markets differ","Select and tailor the approach to the initiative context, including regulatory variation, governance, and stakeholder access","Let each local team define its own approach without shared coordination"],"answer":[2],"why":["A consistent baseline may help, but identical activities can fail to account for material contextual differences.","Variation alone does not determine the approach; assess uncertainty, governance, risks, and constraints.","Correct: tailor the approach to relevant context while retaining appropriate coordination across the initiative.","Local input matters, but uncoordinated approaches can create gaps and incompatible outputs."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach, printed pp. 24–30","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q222","domains":[3],"level":1,"tag":"Context","q":"A BA plan assumes subject matter experts can attend weekly workshops. A new operating restriction limits them to short monthly sessions. What is the most appropriate response?","opts":["Reassess the approach and adapt elicitation timing or techniques to the experts' availability and the information need","Keep the plan and ask the sponsor to make the experts available","Replace expert participation with document analysis for every topic","Stop planning until the restriction is removed"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: a changed constraint can affect the approach; adapt the relevant activities and techniques while preserving the information objectives.","Sponsor support may help, but the BA should assess workable options rather than assume the restriction can be overridden.","Document analysis may suit some topics, but it cannot automatically replace expert knowledge for every information need.","The restriction is a planning input, not necessarily a reason to suspend all analysis."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach, printed pp. 24–30","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q223","domains":[3],"level":1,"tag":"Context","q":"A requirement package contains public process details and restricted customer data. Several teams need access to different portions. Which information-management choice best fits the context?","opts":["Define classification, ownership, access, retention, and controlled links or views suited to each team's need","Create separate unmanaged copies for each team","Give all project participants access because the package supports the same initiative","Remove restricted data from the package without recording where it came from"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: define how information is classified, accessed, maintained, and related in a way that reflects the initiative's context.","Unmanaged copies can diverge and weaken control over changes and access.","Common project membership does not establish a need or authorization for all information.","Removing data without trace or relationship may damage meaning and prevent authorized analysis."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management, printed pp. 42–46","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q224","domains":[3],"level":1,"tag":"Context","q":"A BA wants to reuse a process model created for a different division. Which check is most important before treating it as current analysis information?","opts":["Whether the model is visually clear","Whether the previous division approved it","Whether the model contains a complete list of activities","Whether its source, currency, assumptions, and applicability match the present division's context"],"answer":[3],"why":["Clarity helps communication but does not establish that the model fits this division.","Approval in another context does not demonstrate current applicability.","A complete activity list may still reflect different roles, policies, or conditions.","Correct: assess relevance, currency, provenance, assumptions, and fit before reusing information in a new context."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §3.4 Plan Business Analysis Information Management, printed pp. 42–46","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q225","domains":[3],"level":2,"tag":"Context","q":"A requirement remains unchanged, but the business objective it supports has been revised. What should the BA do to maintain the requirement?","opts":["Keep the record as it is because the requirement wording has not changed","Rewrite the requirement immediately to match the new objective","Review and update its relevant relationships and context information, preserving its history and assessing continued relevance","Delete the requirement because its original objective no longer exists"],"answer":[2],"why":["The unchanged wording does not mean its context and relationships remain accurate.","The new objective may not require different wording; analyze the relationship before editing the requirement.","Correct: maintain requirement attributes and relationships as context changes, and determine whether the requirement remains relevant.","The requirement may still support another objective or need; deletion should follow analysis and governance."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.2 Maintain Requirements, printed pp. 83–85","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q226","domains":[3],"level":2,"tag":"Context","q":"A team keeps replacing the current requirement with revised text, making it difficult to tell what stakeholders reviewed. Which maintenance practice best preserves context?","opts":["Maintain version and status history and link the current requirement to relevant prior decisions","Store only the latest text and keep approval emails separately","Create a new requirement for every wording change without linking versions","Mark all previous wording as obsolete without retaining its relationships"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: preserve the evolution and relationships of the requirement so reviewers can understand which version and context informed decisions.","Separate emails may be difficult to reconcile with the current item and can leave context fragmented.","Unlinked copies obscure continuity and may create uncertainty about the active requirement.","A superseded version may still be needed to understand decisions, impacts, and traceability."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.2 Maintain Requirements, printed pp. 83–85","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q227","domains":[3],"level":2,"tag":"Context","q":"A requirement set was approved for a limited pilot. The team now proposes expanding it to all regions, where operating rules differ. What should the BA confirm?","opts":["That the wording has not changed, which makes the original approval sufficient","That the delivery manager supports the expansion","The scope and context covered by the approval, and whether the wider use requires further review or approval","That the pilot met its schedule before applying the requirements broadly"],"answer":[2],"why":["Unchanged wording does not mean the earlier approval covered a different geographic or operating context.","Delivery support does not necessarily establish the approval authority or stakeholder acceptance.","Correct: determine what was approved and assess whether expanded applicability falls within that decision and process.","Schedule performance does not establish approval scope or suitability across regions."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.5 Approve Requirements, printed pp. 95–102","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q228","domains":[3],"level":2,"tag":"Context","q":"A requirement was approved by a product owner for one release. A later release changes its applicability but not its wording. What is the best way to record its approval status?","opts":["Carry forward approval automatically because the text is identical","Mark it approved for the entire product because the owner approved it once","Treat it as rejected until a new approval meeting occurs","Record the scope of the original approval and determine under the agreed process whether the changed applicability needs a new decision"],"answer":[3],"why":["Approval applies to a decision and context; identical text alone does not establish broader scope.","One release approval does not automatically authorize every later use.","A change in applicability does not itself mean the requirement was rejected.","Correct: keep the approval scope clear and follow the agreed process to determine whether a new decision is needed."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.5 Approve Requirements, printed pp. 95–102","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q229","domains":[3],"level":3,"tag":"Context","q":"A new service is proposed after a merger. Stakeholders disagree whether the current problem is duplicate work, unclear accountability, or incompatible data. What should the BA do first?","opts":["Select the issue with the largest estimated cost","Analyze the current state across relevant processes, information, roles, and organizational conditions to understand how the issues relate","Define the future state and let it reveal which problem matters","Use the acquirer's operating model as the current-state baseline"],"answer":[1],"why":["Cost estimates may help prioritize later but do not establish causes or how the problems interact.","Correct: analyze current-state elements and their relationships in the merger context before defining the change.","The future state should respond to an understood need; it is not a substitute for current-state analysis.","The acquirer's model may inform options but does not necessarily describe the combined enterprise's current state."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §6.1 Analyze Current State, printed pp. 103–109","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q230","domains":[3],"level":3,"tag":"Context","q":"A process is reported to be slow, but the delay occurs only when a downstream system is unavailable. What current-state evidence is most useful?","opts":["Average completion time across all cases","Interviews with process owners only","The process diagram approved before the system outage","Performance and event data segmented by system availability, together with stakeholder input about the operating conditions"],"answer":[3],"why":["Averages can hide the condition associated with the delay.","Owner input is useful but may not show when and how the outage affects performance.","A prior diagram may not reflect current operating conditions or actual performance.","Correct: analyze performance in its operational context and combine relevant data with stakeholder understanding of the conditions."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §6.1 Analyze Current State, printed pp. 103–109","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q231","domains":[3],"level":3,"tag":"Context","q":"A requirement is understandable in isolation, but its meaning changes depending on which customer type is processed. What should the BA do during verification?","opts":["Clarify the applicable context or conditions so the requirement can be interpreted consistently","Accept it as verified because each customer type has a known process","Ask the sponsor to approve the ambiguity","Move the distinction into the solution design and leave the requirement unchanged"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: verify clarity and precision by stating relevant conditions or contexts that affect interpretation.","Known processes do not ensure the requirement communicates which condition applies.","Approval does not resolve ambiguity in the requirement.","Moving business meaning into design may leave the requirement unclear and constrain solution alternatives."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.2 Verify Requirements, printed pp. 141–143","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q232","domains":[3],"level":3,"tag":"Context","q":"Two requirements use the term “account” differently: one means a customer profile, the other a financial ledger. What is the most appropriate verification action?","opts":["Accept both because each is clear to its own author","Ask the solution team to choose a shared meaning during implementation","Identify and resolve the terminology ambiguity in the relevant context, then ensure the requirements use or define terms consistently","Delete one requirement because the same term cannot appear twice"],"answer":[2],"why":["Author familiarity does not ensure that intended readers will interpret the term consistently.","Implementation should not be left to infer business meaning that can be clarified during analysis.","Correct: verify terminology and context so the requirements communicate distinct or shared meanings unambiguously.","The requirements may express different concepts; the ambiguity should be resolved, not handled by automatic deletion."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.2 Verify Requirements, printed pp. 141–143","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q233","domains":[3],"level":4,"tag":"Context","q":"A solution spans a customer portal, a case-management system, and a regulatory reporting service. Stakeholders understand requirements by component, but value depends on an end-to-end customer journey. What should the BA consider?","opts":["Keep only component-based groupings because they match system ownership","Create a single flat list so every requirement has equal visibility","Move cross-system relationships into the implementation plan","Structure requirements and relationships to support both component views and the end-to-end context stakeholders need"],"answer":[3],"why":["Component views are useful but may hide dependencies across the customer journey.","A flat list can make structure and relationships harder to understand.","Implementation planning does not replace requirements architecture or communicate their relationships during analysis.","Correct: organize requirements and relationships to reflect relevant stakeholder perspectives and solution context."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.4 Define Requirements Architecture, printed pp. 148–151","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q234","domains":[3],"level":4,"tag":"Context","q":"An enterprise requirement affects several capabilities, while each project team owns only one capability. How should the requirements architecture help?","opts":["Show relevant relationships and dependencies across capability boundaries while preserving useful ownership views","Assign the enterprise requirement to whichever team has the earliest release","Break it into local requirements and remove the relationship to the enterprise need","Keep only the enterprise requirement because lower-level requirements create duplication"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: architecture should make relationships across the solution visible while supporting views that help stakeholders understand ownership and context.","Release timing does not determine the requirement's meaning or cross-capability relationships.","Decomposition may be useful, but removing its relationship to the enterprise requirement loses context.","Lower-level requirements may express necessary detail; assess relationships rather than assuming duplication."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.4 Define Requirements Architecture, printed pp. 148–151","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q235","domains":[3],"level":4,"tag":"Context","q":"A solution meets its specification but cannot support a stakeholder's required data format. A costly adapter could address the gap. How should the BA assess the issue?","opts":["Classify it as an enterprise limitation because the stakeholder uses another format","Assess the solution limitation, its effect on value in this context, and the cost and feasibility of possible responses","Treat it as a requirement defect because the solution does not support the format","Ignore the gap if the adapter can technically be built"],"answer":[1],"why":["The stakeholder context affects the impact, but the stated capability gap is a limitation of the solution.","Correct: assess how the solution limitation affects value and compare viable responses, including their costs and constraints.","A requirement may need review, but that does not change the solution limitation being assessed.","Technical feasibility alone does not establish that an adapter is affordable or worthwhile."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.3 Assess Solution Limitations, printed pp. 173–176","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q236","domains":[3],"level":4,"tag":"Context","q":"The same solution performs well in a pilot but fails under the volume and network conditions of a remote location. What should the BA examine?","opts":["Whether the pilot results were formally approved","Whether the remote location should be excluded from the solution scope","How the solution limitation interacts with local operating conditions and affects value, including possible mitigations","Whether the performance target should be lowered to match the remote results"],"answer":[2],"why":["Approval of pilot results does not show that the solution performs in a different context.","Scope exclusion is a possible decision but requires understanding the need and impact first.","Correct: assess the limitation under the conditions where it occurs and evaluate its effect on value and possible responses.","Lowering a target without analysis may conceal a gap rather than address its cause or stakeholder impact."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.3 Assess Solution Limitations, printed pp. 173–176","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q237","domains":[3],"level":5,"tag":"Context","q":"A newly acquired business unit uses different definitions, decision rights, and data sources. A combined performance measure appears worse than either unit's prior result. What is the best current-state analysis?","opts":["Adopt the legacy unit's measure because it has the longest history","Compare the measure definitions and operating contexts, then establish a meaningful baseline for the combined enterprise","Average the two historical results to create a neutral baseline","Treat the worse result as evidence that the merger change has failed"],"answer":[1],"why":["History is useful but the legacy definition may not fit the combined enterprise context.","Correct: understand differences in definitions, sources, and conditions before interpreting performance or setting a baseline.","A simple average may combine noncomparable measures and produce a misleading baseline.","A worse combined measure may reflect changed definitions or context; analyze before drawing a conclusion."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §6.1 Analyze Current State, printed pp. 103–109","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q238","domains":[3],"level":5,"tag":"Context","q":"A solution could create value, but an enterprise-wide policy prevents one region from sharing the required data. What should the BA assess?","opts":["Whether the solution can be changed to bypass the policy","The enterprise limitation's source, scope, effect on potential value, and feasible ways to address or accommodate it","Whether the data-sharing requirement should be removed from every region","Whether the policy is outdated, and therefore can be ignored during assessment"],"answer":[1],"why":["Changing the solution may not address a policy constraint and could create additional risk.","Correct: assess the enterprise condition and its impact on value, then consider feasible responses in the relevant context.","A regional constraint does not necessarily justify removing a requirement for all regions.","A policy remains a constraint until changed through appropriate authority; it should not be ignored."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations, printed pp. 177–181","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q239","domains":[3],"level":5,"tag":"Context","q":"A solution's value is constrained by a vendor contract, an internal approval delay, and limited specialist capacity. Which assessment best supports a useful recommendation?","opts":["Treat all three as solution defects because they reduce benefits","Focus on the vendor contract because it is external to the enterprise","Treat only specialist capacity as a limitation because it affects operations","Distinguish the enterprise limitations by source and context, assess their combined effect on value, and compare feasible responses"],"answer":[3],"why":["These conditions may constrain value without being defects in the solution itself.","The internal constraints may be equally or more important to value realization than the vendor condition.","Approval delays and contractual terms can also limit value, even though they are not capacity constraints.","Correct: identify enterprise limitations, understand how they interact, and assess realistic responses in context."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations, printed pp. 177–181","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q240","domains":[3],"level":5,"tag":"Context","q":"A department can remove a policy barrier, but doing so shifts risk to another department and requires new training. How should the BA frame the enterprise limitation?","opts":["As resolved once the first department agrees to change its policy","As a constraint whose potential removal and side effects must be assessed across affected parts of the enterprise","As a solution limitation because training is required","As outside the business analysis scope because it involves organizational policy"],"answer":[1],"why":["Agreement by one department does not establish that the barrier is removed or that side effects are acceptable.","Correct: assess the limitation and the consequences of addressing it across affected stakeholders and enterprise areas.","Training needs do not make the policy constraint a limitation of the solution.","Organizational policy can constrain value and is relevant to assessing enterprise limitations."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations, printed pp. 177–181","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-22.html"},{"id":"q241","domains":[9],"level":1,"tag":"Applying standards in context","q":"A global organization wants to use the Standard to improve consistency. One unit is heavily regulated, another is exploring an uncertain product opportunity, and a third is making a small operational change. What is the best way to apply the Standard?","opts":["Require each unit to use the same tasks and approach in the same order","Use the Standard as foundational guidance and tailor its application to each initiative's context and need","Use it only for the regulated unit and rely on local practices elsewhere","Select one approach for all units, then adjust stakeholder communication by region"],"answer":[1],"why":["The Standard does not prescribe a fixed sequence or one approach for every context.","Correct. The Standard provides foundational guidance and says it should be applied with consideration for the context and need of each initiative.","The Standard applies across contexts; the level and form of application may vary, but it is not limited to regulated work.","Approach selection depends on the characteristics of the work, not only location or communication needs."],"ref":"Chapter 1, §1.1, p. 1","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-5.html"},{"id":"q242","domains":[5],"level":2,"tag":"Standard and detailed guidance","q":"A study group uses the Standard to understand task purposes, then needs detailed task guidance and examples to prepare for certification. Which next step is most consistent with the Standard?","opts":["Use the Standard task cards as the full certification syllabus","Study only the knowledge area diagram because task purposes are already known","Use the BABOK Guide and appropriate certification resources for detailed study, keeping the Standard as an overview","Replace the Standard with a technique catalog because it has no certification relevance"],"answer":[2],"why":["The Standard is a foundational overview, not a complete certification syllabus; the preface directs candidates to detailed resources.","The Standard says to use the BABOK Guide for comprehensive knowledge area and task detail, rather than relying only on a diagram.","Correct. The Standard is a starting point and gateway; its preface recommends the BABOK Guide and appropriate certification handbooks for study detail.","The Standard is relevant as a foundation and overview, even though detailed exam preparation requires other resources."],"ref":"Chapter 1, §§1.5–1.7, pp. 2–3","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-5.html"},{"id":"q243","domains":[7],"level":3,"tag":"BACCM and value","q":"A service redesign improves average completion time, but customers with accessibility needs report worse outcomes. The team has discussed the solution and metrics but not how stakeholders and context affect value. What is the best analytical next step?","opts":["Add an accessibility requirement immediately and treat the rest of the analysis as complete","Compare the completion-time metric with the project budget and decide whether the gain is sufficient","Retain the solution because an improved average indicates that value has been delivered","Use the BACCM to examine how stakeholders and context affect the relationship among the need, solution, change, and value"],"answer":[3],"why":["An accessibility need may need to be addressed, but one requirement alone does not complete the analysis of stakeholder outcomes.","Budget is relevant but does not address stakeholder impacts or contextual differences in value.","An improved average can hide worse outcomes for a stakeholder group and does not by itself demonstrate value for all.","Correct. The BACCM helps analyze how stakeholder needs and context relate to the solution, change, and value; an average metric alone can hide different stakeholder outcomes."],"ref":"Chapter 2, §§2.1–2.2, pp. 5–6","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-5.html"},{"id":"q244","domains":[4],"level":1,"tag":"Problem understanding","q":"A call centre's average handling time has risen. Leaders propose replacing its software, but the analyst finds that the increase began after a policy change and is concentrated in one call type. What should the analyst do before recommending a solution?","opts":["Investigate the underlying cause and context of the increase","Prioritize the software replacement based on the average handling time","Define the future state around a lower average handling time","Measure the replacement software after it is implemented"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct. Business analysis should move beyond symptoms to understand underlying causes and context before recommending targeted solutions.","Prioritization cannot establish whether the software is the cause or the right response.","A future-state objective can be defined after understanding the need and its cause; a single metric may not capture the underlying issue.","Measurement is important after implementation, but it does not replace analysis before committing to a solution."],"ref":"Chapter 2, §2.4, p. 7","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-5.html"},{"id":"q245","domains":[5],"level":2,"tag":"Need and solution","q":"A department asks for an automated approval tool. Early discussion reveals that approvals are delayed because decision rights are unclear, and some approvals may not be necessary. What is the best business analysis response?","opts":["Document the tool as a stakeholder requirement because a department requested it","Clarify the problem or opportunity first, then assess which solution or combination of changes could satisfy the need","Treat automation as the need and compare vendors against the requested features","Reject the request because a process change would be more efficient than technology"],"answer":[1],"why":["The requested tool is a proposed solution, not necessarily the underlying stakeholder requirement or need.","Correct. A need is the problem or opportunity to address; solutions should be considered after clarifying it and may include process or technology changes.","Comparing vendors before clarifying the need risks automating unnecessary or poorly governed work.","A process change may be an option, but it should be evaluated alongside other ways to satisfy the clarified need."],"ref":"Chapter 2, §2.1, p. 5","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-5.html"},{"id":"q246","domains":[5],"level":2,"tag":"Think as a customer","q":"A team is designing a claims portal. It has internal feature requests, but customer research shows that people mainly need to understand claim status and what action to take next. Which principle should guide how the team elaborates the emerging solution?","opts":["See the whole, by analyzing the enterprise context and all impacted stakeholders","Analyze to determine what is valuable, by prioritizing work against value","Think as a customer, by translating high-level customer needs into detailed understanding that shapes the solution","Avoid waste, by removing features that do not appear in the current backlog"],"answer":[2],"why":["Seeing the whole is useful for broader context, but the scenario emphasizes how customer needs should shape the solution.","Value prioritization is relevant, but the primary principle is understanding customer experience and evolving the solution from customer needs.","Correct. Think as a customer starts with high-level customer needs and develops detailed understanding to evolve the emerging solution.","Avoiding waste can remove non-contributing activity, but a feature's absence from a backlog is not evidence that it is waste."],"ref":"Chapter 3, §3.3, pp. 10–11","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-5.html"},{"id":"q247","domains":[4],"level":3,"tag":"Problem solving","q":"A proposed change reduces one visible complaint, but the same failure keeps appearing in other channels. The analyst traces the issue to a shared process cause and reframes the need around that cause. Which competency is most directly demonstrated?","opts":["Facilitation, because the analyst is aligning stakeholders around a new statement","Systems thinking, because the analyst is considering related channels","Adaptability, because the analyst is changing the initial scope","Problem solving, because the analyst is reframing the need to address the recurring root cause"],"answer":[3],"why":["Facilitation may help stakeholders agree, but the defining action is investigating why the issue recurs.","Systems thinking can help reveal relationships across channels, but the competency in focus is addressing the recurring root cause.","Adaptability may help the analyst adjust, but the competency most directly represented is addressing the root cause.","Correct. The Standard connects problem solving with ensuring created value addresses the root cause of problems."],"ref":"Chapter 3, §3.4, p. 12","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-5.html"},{"id":"q248","domains":[4],"level":1,"tag":"See the whole","q":"An inventory change reduces warehouse handling time but increases stockouts at retail locations and makes supplier forecasts less reliable. Which principle is most useful for reviewing the proposed change?","opts":["See the whole, by examining the context, solution, stakeholders, and connected outcomes","Get real using examples, by validating the warehouse team's estimate","Understand what is doable, by checking warehouse capacity","Think as a customer, by focusing on retailer satisfaction alone"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct. Seeing the whole means understanding the broader context and the relationships among solution, stakeholders, and desired outcomes.","Examples can validate assumptions, but the issue is the connected effects across the enterprise.","Feasibility matters, but the proposal is already feasible in the warehouse; the cross-system consequences need review.","Retailer satisfaction matters, but the principle calls for a broader view that includes suppliers and other impacted stakeholders."],"ref":"Chapter 3, §3.3, pp. 10–11","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-5.html"},{"id":"q249","domains":[5],"level":2,"tag":"Approach selection","q":"A program includes a regulated data conversion with stable requirements and a new customer service that must be refined through feedback. The sponsor asks whether one approach can cover the entire initiative. What is the best recommendation?","opts":["Use predictive throughout because the data conversion is regulated","Use adaptive throughout because the new service is uncertain","Use a hybrid approach, applying predictive and adaptive work to the parts where they fit","Delay planning until both parts have fully specified requirements"],"answer":[2],"why":["Predictive fits the stable conversion, but may not support the learning needed for the uncertain service.","Adaptive fits the uncertain service, but the stable migration may benefit from predictive planning.","Correct. A hybrid approach can use predictive and adaptive methods in different parts of the same initiative.","Planning can begin with current understanding and be refined; waiting for full specification defeats the purpose of selecting an approach suited to uncertainty."],"ref":"Chapter 4, §§4.2.1–4.2.3, pp. 14–15","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-5.html"},{"id":"q250","domains":[4],"level":3,"tag":"Requirement classification","q":"A new portal must let customers change delivery dates. It must also respond within two seconds, while a temporary data-conversion capability is needed for launch. How should these needs be classified?","opts":["The date change is a non-functional requirement; response time is functional; conversion is stakeholder","The date change is functional; response time is non-functional; conversion is a transition requirement","The date change is a business requirement; response time is transition; conversion is non-functional","All three are stakeholder requirements because customers and staff are affected"],"answer":[1],"why":["This reverses the distinctions: the date-change capability is functional, the response-time quality is non-functional, and conversion is a transition need.","Correct. Changing a date is a functional capability, response time is a non-functional quality, and temporary conversion supports transition from current to future state.","This misclassifies the response time and conversion; the temporary capability supports transition.","Stakeholder requirements represent stakeholder needs, but these items are described at different requirement levels and should be classified by their role."],"ref":"Chapter 4, §4.4.2, p. 17","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-5.html"},{"id":"q251","domains":[2],"level":3,"tag":"Ethical analysis","q":"An automated eligibility model can reduce processing time. Testing also suggests it may disadvantage a stakeholder group, and the vendor says the model's decision logic is proprietary. What is the most appropriate business analysis response?","opts":["Recommend the model because faster processing is the primary measurable benefit","Ask the vendor to address fairness after the model is implemented","Treat fairness as a technical limitation and exclude it from the value assessment","Assess whether stakeholder needs are ethically met, consider value and risks, and involve appropriate stakeholders before recommending"],"answer":[3],"why":["Efficiency is one potential benefit, but it does not resolve the ethical concern or stakeholder impact.","The Standard emphasizes addressing ethical needs during analysis rather than deferring them until after implementation.","Ethical considerations are part of business analysis and value assessment, not solely a technical matter.","Correct. The Standard emphasizes ethically meeting stakeholder needs while considering value, and making decisions consistent with ethical guidance."],"ref":"Chapter 4, §4.5.2, p. 18","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-5.html"},{"id":"q252","domains":[5],"level":2,"tag":"Requirements and designs","q":"A prototype reveals that users interpret a requirement differently from the analyst's original wording. The team has not yet approved the design. What is the best response?","opts":["Keep the requirement fixed and adjust the prototype to match its wording","Use the design feedback to refine the requirement and design together, preserving their distinct purposes","Replace the requirement with the prototype because the design is more concrete","Pause design work until every requirement is complete and approved"],"answer":[1],"why":["Keeping the wording fixed may preserve a misunderstanding revealed by the prototype review.","Correct. Requirements and designs are interdependent and cyclical; feedback from a design can refine the requirement, and both can be updated while retaining their distinct purposes.","A design represents a potential solution and does not replace the requirement's representation of the need.","The Standard does not require all requirements to be complete before design work; the two can inform one another."],"ref":"Chapter 4, §4.4.1, p. 16","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-5.html"},{"id":"q253","domains":[7],"level":1,"tag":"Stakeholder engagement","q":"A change affects customers, regulators, and operations. The analyst knows some stakeholders can influence decisions while others will experience the greatest impact. What should the engagement approach establish?","opts":["Relevant stakeholders, their roles and characteristics, potential impacts, and suitable collaboration and communication","A single workshop schedule and one communication channel for every stakeholder","A list of approvers, excluding groups without formal decision rights","The elicitation techniques for each requirement, without recording stakeholder relationships"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct. Stakeholder engagement planning identifies stakeholders and their roles, characteristics, impacts, and how to collaborate and communicate with them.","A single approach may not meet different stakeholder needs; the engagement approach should consider their characteristics and communication needs.","Approvers are important, but stakeholders include people who are impacted or can influence the change even without formal authority.","Elicitation techniques are planned for activities; stakeholder relationships and engagement need broader consideration."],"ref":"Chapter 5, Plan Stakeholder Engagement, p. 24","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-5.html"},{"id":"q254","domains":[7],"level":2,"tag":"Validate requirements and designs","q":"A requirement set is clear, internally consistent, and detailed enough to use. Business stakeholders now need to confirm that it supports objectives and will deliver the needed value. Which task is needed next?","opts":["Verify Requirements and Designs again, because quality must be checked before value","Approve Requirements and Designs, because stakeholders must authorize them before review","Validate Requirements and Designs, because alignment and expected value are being assessed","Trace Requirements and Designs, because objectives must be linked to requirements"],"answer":[2],"why":["Verification checks quality and usability; the scenario says those checks are already satisfied and now asks about alignment and value.","Approval may follow validation, but authorization alone does not establish that the requirements support objectives and value.","Correct. Validation ensures requirements and designs align with business requirements and support needed value.","Traceability can show relationships to objectives, but it does not substitute for stakeholder validation of value and alignment."],"ref":"Chapter 5, Validate Requirements and Designs, p. 44; Verify Requirements and Designs, p. 43","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-5.html"},{"id":"q255","domains":[8],"level":3,"tag":"Assess risks","q":"A transition plan depends on a supplier delivering data on time. The supplier's schedule is uncertain, and a delay could disrupt operations and reduce expected value. What should the analyst do?","opts":["Define the future state again because supplier timing is uncertain","Approve the requirements and monitor the supplier during delivery","Change the strategy immediately to a full replacement launch","Assess the risk, understand its potential consequences, and recommend an appropriate response"],"answer":[3],"why":["The desired future state may remain valid; the uncertainty is about a potential consequence during transition.","Approval does not analyze or treat the supplier timing uncertainty.","A full replacement launch is a strategy choice, not a response supported by analysis of this uncertainty.","Correct. Assess Risks clarifies uncertainty, considers its effect on value delivery, and recommends actions where appropriate."],"ref":"Chapter 5, Assess Risks, p. 40","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-5.html"},{"id":"q256","domains":[4],"level":1,"tag":"Define change strategy","q":"The organization agrees on its current and desired future states. It is comparing incremental transitions with one large transformation and needs a recommended route between the two states. Which task is most directly responsible?","opts":["Define Change Strategy","Define Future State","Analyze Current State","Specify and Model Requirements and Designs"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct. Define Change Strategy assesses alternative approaches to change and recommends how to move from current to future state, including transition states.","This task establishes the desired conditions; the future state is already agreed in the scenario.","This task describes and analyzes the existing state; that work is already complete.","This task develops requirements and designs, not the overall transition route."],"ref":"Chapter 5, Define Change Strategy, p. 41","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-5.html"}]);